WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
162 billion kilowatt hours of lignite, the most since reunification, came from Germany in 2013 despite record renewables. This is unintended consequences, Episode 180. German lawmakers passed the Renewable Energy Sources Act in 2000 to accelerate wind, solar and biomass after years of slow progress under earlier support schemes. They had watched Denmark's early wind programs and wanted a reliable mechanism that would guarantee investors a fixed price for every kilowatt hour.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:36.120
are fed into the grid. The goal was straightforward. Replace fossil and nuclear generation with renewables

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.580
as quickly as possible while keeping the lights on. Wholesale electricity prices were high

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:46.800
enough at the time that adding power with zero marginal cost was expected to lower consumer

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:52.760
bills over the long run. Policy makers also believed the nuclear phase out agreed after Fukushima

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:58.700
would be covered by the growing renewable fleet. Klaudia Kemfert then at the German Institute for

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:04.100
economic research, later noted that the missing piece was an adequate price on carbon dioxide.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.160
The architects focused on deployment volume rather than on the relative operating costs

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:10.840
of the remaining thermal plants.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.200
An objection often raised later is whether those lawmakers should have foreseen the interaction

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.820
with the European Union emissions trading system, the ETS.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.240
The ETS price had been designed separately and was expected to tighten on its own schedule.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:30.360
In practice, the two instruments operated on different timelines and different price signals.

00:01:31.375 --> 00:01:37.835
The feed-in tariff system expanded rapidly after 2000 and again after 2009, when solar tariffs

00:01:37.835 --> 00:01:38.735
were still generous.

00:01:39.355 --> 00:01:46.615
By 2013, renewables supplied 152.4 billion kilowatt hours, up from far lower figures a decade

00:01:46.615 --> 00:01:50.295
earlier, and 24.1% of gross electricity.

00:01:50.295 --> 00:01:56.175
property. Proponents pointed to falling technology costs and rising public acceptance. Skeptics

00:01:56.175 --> 00:02:00.755
inside the utility sector warned that the merit order effect would eventually push conventional

00:02:00.755 --> 00:02:06.215
plants out of the money, but few predicted which plants would be displaced first. The ETS

00:02:06.215 --> 00:02:11.935
price had already fallen below 10 euros per ton by 2011 and hovered near 7 euros in

00:02:11.935 --> 00:02:18.355
2013. At the same time, cheap United States coal displaced by domestic shell gas flooded

00:02:18.355 --> 00:02:23.795
European markets. These price signals were visible in day-ahead markets but were not part of the

00:02:23.795 --> 00:02:30.175
original renewable support design. A further complication was that Lignite plants once built had very

00:02:30.175 --> 00:02:36.215
low variable costs because the fuel sat in open pit mines next to the stations. Even modest wholesale

00:02:36.215 --> 00:02:41.255
price drops left those stations profitable while gas plants with pipeline fuel costs did not.

00:02:41.255 --> 00:02:46.646
Agora Energy Vendus April 2014 report documented what followed.

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:51.046
A fuel switch from gas to coal inside Germany and in neighboring countries.

00:02:51.646 --> 00:02:56.826
Renewables have zero marginal cost, so renewables set the clearing price in more hours and drove

00:02:56.826 --> 00:02:58.066
wholesale prices down.

00:02:58.806 --> 00:03:03.566
Gas plants with higher fuel costs became unprofitable first and reduced output.

00:03:04.326 --> 00:03:08.846
Lignite and hard coal plants, whose fuel and carbon costs were lower, remained in the

00:03:08.846 --> 00:03:10.266
money and increased generation.

00:03:10.266 --> 00:03:15.866
Patric gritchin of Agora Energy Venda told reporters that German coal plants were displacing

00:03:15.866 --> 00:03:18.946
gas plants not only domestically but also in the Netherlands.

00:03:19.726 --> 00:03:25.086
Carbon dioxide emissions rose in both 2012 and 2013 despite the renewable growth.

00:03:25.566 --> 00:03:28.286
The nuclear phase out itself did not drive the increase.

00:03:29.066 --> 00:03:34.086
Agora's analysis showed that renewable additions had fully offset the lost nuclear generation.

00:03:34.526 --> 00:03:39.826
The second order effect was record electricity exports, much of that power coal fired, which

00:03:39.826 --> 00:03:41.526
further raised domestic emissions.

00:03:42.326 --> 00:03:46.766
Economists noted that the ETS price was simply too low to change the relative cost of

00:03:46.766 --> 00:03:47.846
lignite versus gas.

00:03:48.506 --> 00:03:52.466
The nuclear phase out gap does not, on the numbers, explain the coal surge.

00:03:53.206 --> 00:03:54.446
Renewables covered that gap.

00:03:55.006 --> 00:03:59.526
The additional coal burn came from the change dispatch order among the remaining thermal fleet.

00:03:59.986 --> 00:04:04.766
The third order effect was that utilities kept older lignite units online longer because

00:04:04.766 --> 00:04:09.266
those units still cleared the market, delaying any early retirement that higher carbon

00:04:09.266 --> 00:04:16.681
prices might have triggered. By late 2014 the picture began to shift. Renewables reached 25.8%

00:04:16.681 --> 00:04:21.181
of gross generation and became the single largest source for the first time, ahead of

00:04:21.181 --> 00:04:28.301
lignite at 25.6%. Total emissions fell roughly 5% that year as milder weather and further renewable

00:04:28.301 --> 00:04:34.681
growth reduced coal and gas burn. Policy makers responded with the 2014 reform of the Renewable

00:04:34.681 --> 00:04:38.961
Energy Sources Act, which slowed tariff digression and introduced auctions.

00:04:39.481 --> 00:04:44.741
Patrick Grichen argued that a tighter emissions cap or a national carbon price floor would

00:04:44.741 --> 00:04:46.641
still be needed to force coal reductions.

00:04:47.261 --> 00:04:49.881
No immediate reversal of the feed-in tariff occurred.

00:04:50.461 --> 00:04:55.641
The system evolved toward market-based procurement while the underlying merit-order dynamic remained.

00:04:56.201 --> 00:05:01.141
The reform addressed deployment speed but left the carbon price gap intact, so the same

00:05:01.141 --> 00:05:05.601
Some relative costs logic continue to favor lignite whenever gas prices state high.

00:05:06.483 --> 00:05:11.563
When a support rule rewards only the volume of zero marginal cost generation without adjusting

00:05:11.563 --> 00:05:16.223
the carbon price that governs dispatch, the plants with the lowest running costs among

00:05:16.223 --> 00:05:17.963
the remaining fleet keep operating.

00:05:18.603 --> 00:05:23.543
The same price drop that makes renewables competitive can also extend the life of the cheapest fossil

00:05:23.543 --> 00:05:25.643
plants if their carbon cost stays low.

00:05:26.323 --> 00:05:30.783
Any new clean energy mandate should therefore be paired with an explicit adjustment to the

00:05:30.783 --> 00:05:36.203
relative cost of the dirtiest remaining resources, or the displacement will favor those resources

00:05:36.203 --> 00:05:38.343
over cleaner but more expensive ones.

00:05:38.983 --> 00:05:44.123
Capacity markets pay for availability, and clean energy standards pay for clean megawatt

00:05:44.123 --> 00:05:49.343
hours, yet neither instrument automatically checks which remaining plant becomes more profitable

00:05:49.343 --> 00:05:52.163
once zero marginal cost power floods the stack.

00:05:52.823 --> 00:05:57.123
Environmental intelligence is where this network stays with the carbon price and procurement

00:05:57.123 --> 00:05:59.603
rules a mandate like that keeps running into.

00:05:59.603 --> 00:06:04.183
How might today's capacity markets or clean energy standards change if they

00:06:04.183 --> 00:06:09.243
incorporated that same relative cost test? That's the case for today. The lessons

00:06:09.243 --> 00:06:12.943
tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone who'd

00:06:12.943 --> 00:06:17.443
find it useful, and subscribe wherever you're listening. See you tomorrow, and if

00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:21.883
you'd rather watch than listen, find us on YouTube at Narin Network, links in the

00:06:21.883 --> 00:06:26.343
show notes. One more thing, if you like today's episode, our sister show First

